    "snippet": "---\ntitle: Federico Garc\u00eda Lorca - Wikipedia\nauthor: Authority control databases\ndate: 2002-10-06\n---\nname: Federico Garc\u00eda Lorca\nalt: Federico Garc\u00eda Lorca smiling, standing in front of a theatre poster\nbirth_name: Federico del Sagrado Coraz\u00f3n de Jes\u00fas Garc\u00eda Lorca\nbirth_date: 5 June 1898\nbirth_place: Fuente Vaqueros, Granada, Kingdom of Spain\ndeath_date: 19 August 1936\ndeath_place: near Alfacar, Granada, Spanish Republic\neducation: Columbia University University of Granada\noccupation: * Playwright, * poet, * theatre director\nnationality: Spanish\nfather: Federico Garc\u00eda Rodr\u00edguez\nmother: Vicenta Lorca Romero\nmovement: Generation of '27\nsignature: Federico Garc\u00eda Lorca signature.svg\n\nFederico del Sagrado Coraz\u00f3n de Jes\u00fas Garc\u00eda Lorca (5 June 1898 \u2013 19 August 1936) was a Spanish poet, playwright, and theatre director. Garc\u00eda Lorca achieved international recognition as an emblematic member of the Generation of '27, a group consisting mostly of poets who introduced the tenets of European movements (such as symbolism, futurism, and surrealism) into Spanish literature.\"Generation of 1927\". Encyclop\u00e6dia Britannica Online. Encyclop\u00e6dia Britannica Inc., n.d. Web. 18 November 2015\n\nHe initially rose to fame with Romancero gitano (Gypsy Ballads, 1928), a book of poems depicting life in his native Andalusia. His poetry incorporated traditional Andalusian motifs and avant-garde styles. After a sojourn in New York City from 1929 to 1930\u2014documented posthumously in Poeta en Nueva York (Poet in New York, 1942)\u2014he returned to Spain and wrote his best-known plays, Blood Wedding (1932), Yerma (1934), and The House of Bernarda Alba (1936).\n\nGarc\u00eda Lorca was homosexual and suffered from depression after the end of his relationship with sculptor Emilio Aladr\u00e9n Perojo. Garc\u00eda Lorca also had a close emotional relationship for a time with Salvador Dal\u00ed, who said he rejected Garc\u00eda Lorca's sexual advances. \n\nGarc\u00eda Lorca was assassinatedIan Gibson, The Assassination of Federico Garc\u00eda Lorca. Penguin ("
